  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n- Wet, windy, and colder today; Frost tonight\n\nNo surprises thus far with the ongoing system over the area this \nmorning. The main batch of rain showers came through earlier with \nthe nose of the strong low level jet passing through. This has \nexited east, and the initial cold front has just about cleared the \narea to the east as of 230 am. \n\nPlenty of forcing remains and continues to bring showers to the area \nas the large northern stream trough is now approaching with large \nscale upper divergence ahead of it moving over the area. In \naddition, the RRQ of a 140 knot upper jet will aid in the rain \ndevelopment this morning. \n\nWe continue to watch the secondary front/cold conveyor belt that is \nexpected to drop through from mid morning into the early afternoon \nhours. This will bring more showers to the area, highly concentrated \nnear the lakeshore where cold enough low level air and cyclonic \nflow/cold air aloft moves in. We continue to see good signs that a \nfew storms will be possible close to the lakeshore with this \nfeature due to the strong instability over the lake feeding this. \n\nThe flow will quickly shift to become more from the NNE behind this \nfront. We will see the gusty winds behind this, which look to peak \naround 40 mph for a brief time. No wind advisory looks to be needed \nat this time. Even along the lakeshore where it will be slightly \noffshore. We will see a few pop up rain showers continue to be \npossible inland, but nothing looks to be organized.\n\nOnce we lose the diurnal boost of the instability, the threat for \nshowers will drop significantly or end. The upper cyclonic flow will \nexit the area after 00z, allowing ridging to build in through the \nnight. Winds will be up in the evening, but will settle down quite a \nbit overnight. We could decouple well at that time, and see a fair \namount of frost for much of the interior. A frost advisory may be \nneeded, and can be issued by the day shift.\n\n- Tranquil weather for Sat and beyond with moderating temperatures\n\nConfidence continues to increase that we will see an extended period \nof tranquil weather from Saturday through the upcoming week, and \nlikely beyond.\n\nThe increased confidence comes as we see the pattern expected to be \neven more amplified than previously thought, and the favorable \nweather under the upper ridge expected to be over our region. The \nupper trough/low to our west is expected to close off now and slow \nup in trying to move east. This is likely at least partially due to \nthe closed low off of the east coast and upper ridge slowing down \nthe progression of the upper air flow over the CONUS. \n\nThe majority of the time, pops will be near zero with strong \nsubsidence overhead. There is a period on Thursday and Friday where \nthe ridge is temporarily flattened by energy over Canada. These \nshort waves will have no decent moisture to advect in, and little to \nno moisture to start with.  \n\nTemperatures will start out cool on Saturday in the 50s with the \ncool flow from Canada remaining in place immediately behind the \nFriday system. We will see heights build, and a return flow advect \nwarmer air gradually over the area. We are likely to see \ntemperatures approach 70 by mid week, and stay mild through the end \nof the week, with just a slight cool down with the ridge flattened \nbriefly.",
